Now I see the key issue! Look at this part:

```c
if ((ttype & 15) != 0 && (ttype & 128)) {
    ctx->desc[tnum<<4].buf = malloc(nreads);
    ...
}
```

And then:
```c
i = (tnum<<4) | (ttype&15);
if (j >= i)
    goto err;

ctx->desc[i].buf_l = 0;
ctx->desc[i].buf_a = ctx->desc[j].buf_a;
if (ctx->desc[i].buf) free(ctx->desc[i].buf);
ctx->desc[i].buf = malloc(ctx->desc[i].buf_a);
```

The `ttype & 64` path (copy path) does:
1. If `ttype & 128`, increment `tnum` and zero out `desc[tnum<<4]` through `desc[tnum<<4+15]`.
2. If `(ttype & 15) != 0 && (ttype & 128)`, allocate `desc[tnum<<4].buf`.
3. Set `i = (tnum<<4) | (ttype&15)`.
4. Copy from `desc[j]` to `desc[i]`.

The problem: when `ttype & 64` is set but `ttype & 128` is NOT set, `tnum` is NOT incremented. So `i = (tnum<<4) | (ttype&15)` uses the SAME `tnum` as before. If `ttype&15` is different from the previous `ttype&15`, then `i` is a different slot in the same `tnum` block.

But here's the key: when `ttype & 128` is set, `memset(&ctx->desc[tnum<<4], 0, 16*sizeof(ctx->desc[tnum]))` zeroes out the entire block. This means any previously allocated buffers in that block are LEAKED (not freed). But more importantly, if a previous `tnum` block had buffers that were copied to, and then a new `tnum` block is created, the old buffers are leaked.

Wait, but the double-free... Let me think about this more carefully.

The `free_context` function:
```c
static void free_context(name_context *ctx) {
    int i;
    for (i = 0; i < ctx->max_tok*16; i++)
        free(ctx->desc[i].buf);
    free(ctx);
}
```

It frees `desc[0].buf` through `desc[max_tok*16 - 1].buf`. If two `desc[i].buf` pointers are the same, that's a double-free.

Now, in the copy path:
```c
ctx->desc[i].buf_l = 0;
ctx->desc[i].buf_a = ctx->desc[j].buf_a;
if (ctx->desc[i].buf) free(ctx->desc[i].buf);
ctx->desc[i].buf = malloc(ctx->desc[i].buf_a);
if (!ctx->desc[i].buf)
    goto err;
memcpy(ctx->desc[i].buf, ctx->desc[j].buf, ctx->desc[i].buf_a);
```

This frees `desc[i].buf` and allocates a new one. So `desc[i].buf` is always a fresh allocation. No aliasing here.

But wait - what if `i == j`? The check `if (j >= i) goto err;` prevents that. So `j < i`.

Hmm, but what about the case where `ttype & 64` is set, `ttype & 128` is NOT set, and `ttype & 15` is 0? Then `i = (tnum<<4) | 0`. And `j < i`. The copy would copy from `desc[j]` to `desc[tnum<<4]`. But `desc[tnum<<4].buf` might have been allocated in a previous iteration (when `ttype & 128` was set and `ttype & 15 != 0`). So `free(desc[i].buf)` frees the old buffer, and a new one is allocated. Still no aliasing.

OK, let me think about this from a different angle. The `memset(&ctx->desc[tnum<<4], 0, 16*sizeof(ctx->desc[tnum]))` zeroes out the block. But what if `tnum` is incremented, and then a copy operation references `desc[j]` where `j` is in a PREVIOUS block that was NOT zeroed? The `desc[j].buf` would still be valid. And `desc[i].buf` (in the new block) would be NULL (zeroed). So `free(NULL)` is a no-op, and a new buffer is allocated. No aliasing.

Wait, let me look at the `ttype & 64` path again. When `ttype & 128` is set:
```c
tnum++;
if (tnum >= MAX_TOKENS)
    goto err;
ctx->max_tok = tnum+1;
memset(&ctx->desc[tnum<<4], 0, 16*sizeof(ctx->desc[tnum]));
```

`ctx->max_tok = tnum+1`. So `free_context` will free `desc[0]` through `desc[(tnum+1)*16 - 1]`.

Now, if `tnum` is incremented to, say, 1, then `max_tok = 2`, and `free_context` frees `desc[0]` through `desc[31]`. The `memset` zeroed `desc[16]` through `desc[31]`. So `desc[16].buf` through `desc[31].buf` are NULL. And `desc[0].buf` through `desc[15].buf` retain their previous values.

But what if `tnum` is incremented to 1, and then a copy operation sets `i = (1<<4) | (ttype&15)` and copies from `desc[j]` where `j < i`? If `j` is in the range `[0, 15]`, then `desc[j].buf` is from the previous block. The copy allocates a new buffer for `desc[i].buf`. No aliasing.

Hmm, I'm going in circles. Let me look at the actual fix commit more carefully. The fix adds `if (!ctx->desc[j].buf) goto err;`. This means in the vulnerable code, `ctx->desc[j].buf` could be NULL, and `memcpy(ctx->desc[i].buf, NULL, size)` would crash with a NULL deref. But the crash is a double-free, not a NULL deref.

Wait, actually, let me re-read the fix. The fix also adds:
```c
if (clen < 0 || ctx->desc[i].buf_a != ulen)
    goto err;
```

This is important. In the vulnerable code:
```c
ctx->desc[i].buf_a = ulen;
uint64_t usz = ctx->desc[i].buf_a;
clen = uncompress(use_arith, &in[o], sz-o, ctx->desc[i].buf, &usz);
ctx->desc[i].buf_a = usz;
if (clen < 0)
    goto err;
assert(ctx->desc[i].buf_a == ulen);
```

The `uncompress` function calls `arith_decode` or `rans_decode`. In the vulnerable code, `*out_len` is NOT updated in `arith_decode`/`rans_decode`. So `usz` remains `ulen`. But `arith_uncompress_to` might write MORE than `ulen` bytes to the output buffer!

Wait, let me check. In `arith_uncompress_to`:
```c
if (!out) {
    *out_size = osz;
    if (!(out_free = out = malloc(*out_size)))
        return NULL;
} else {
    if (*out_size < osz)
        return NULL;
    *out_size = osz;
}
```

If `out` is provided, `*out_size` (which is `olen`, initialized from `usz = ulen`) must be >= `osz`. If `osz > ulen`, it returns NULL. So `arith_uncompress_to` won't write more than `ulen` bytes.

But wait, what about the `X_STRIPE` path? Let me check:
```c
if (*in & X_STRIPE) {
    ...
    if (!out) {
        ...
    }
    if (ulen != *out_size) {
        free(out_free);
        return NULL;
    }
    ...
    unsigned char *outN = malloc(ulen);
    ...
    for (i = 0; i < N; i++) {
        olen = ulenN[i];
        ...
        if (!arith_uncompress_to(in+c_meta_len, in_size-c_meta_len, outN + idxN[i], &olen)
            || olen != ulenN[i]) {
            free(out_free);
            free(outN);
            return NULL;
        }
        ...
    }
    unstripe(out, outN, ulen, N, idxN);
    free(outN);
    *out_size = ulen;
    return out;
}
```

In the `X_STRIPE` path, `out` is the caller's buffer. `outN` is a temporary buffer. `unstripe` writes `ulen` bytes to `out`. So `out` gets `ulen` bytes. That's fine.

But wait - in the recursive call `arith_uncompress_to(in+c_meta_len, in_size-c_meta_len, outN + idxN[i], &olen)`, `out` is `outN + idxN[i]` (non-NULL). So `out_free` is NULL. And `olen` is `ulenN[i]`. The recursive call checks `if (*out_size < osz) return NULL;`. So `olen >= osz`. If `osz > ulenN[i]`, it returns NULL. Fine.

Hmm, but what about the `do_pack` path in `arith_uncompress_to`? Let me look at it again:

```c
if (do_pack) {
    if (!(tmp_free = tmp = malloc(*out_size)))
        goto err;
    tmp1 = tmp;  // uncompress
    tmp2 = out;  // unpack
}
```

`tmp_free = tmp = malloc(*out_size)`. `*out_size` is `olen`, which was initialized from `usz = ulen`. So `tmp` is allocated with `ulen` bytes.

Then:
```c
if (do_pack) {
    c_meta_size = hts_unpack_meta(in, in_size, *out_size, map, &npacked_sym);
    ...
    unsigned int osz;
    sz = var_get_u32(in, in_end, &osz);
    in += sz;
    in_size -= sz;
    if (osz > tmp1_size)
        goto err;
    tmp1_size = osz;
}
```

`tmp1_size` is initially `*out_size = ulen`. If `osz > ulen`, goto err. So `tmp1_size = osz <= ulen`.

Then:
```c
if (in_size) {
    ...
    tmp1 = order == 1
        ? arith_uncompress_O1(in, in_size, tmp1, tmp1_size)
        : arith_uncompress_O0(in, in_size, tmp1, tmp1_size);
    ...
}
```

`tmp1` is `tmp` (allocated with `ulen` bytes). `arith_uncompress_O0` writes `tmp1_size = osz <= ulen` bytes to `tmp1`. Fine.

Then:
```c
if (do_pack) {
    if (npacked_sym == 1)
        unpacked_sz = tmp1_size;
    if (!hts_unpack(tmp1, tmp1_size, tmp2, unpacked_sz, npacked_sym, map))
        goto err;
    tmp2_size = unpacked_sz;
}
```

`hts_unpack` writes `unpacked_sz` bytes to `tmp2 = out`. `unpacked_sz = osz` (from the earlier `unpacked_sz = osz`). And `osz <= ulen`. So `hts_unpack` writes at most `ulen` bytes to `out`. Fine.

Hmm, but wait. `unpacked_sz` is set to `osz` earlier:
```c
unpacked_sz = osz;
```

And `osz` is read from the input:
```c
unsigned int osz;
sz = var_get_u32(in, in_end, &osz);
in += sz;
in_size -= sz;
if (osz > tmp1_size)
    goto err;
tmp1_size = osz;
```

So `osz <= tmp1_size = ulen`. And `unpacked_sz = osz <= ulen`. So `hts_unpack` writes at most `ulen` bytes to `out`. Fine.

OK, I'm really struggling to find the exact double-free. Let me look at the `decode_name` function's `N_DUP` case more carefully, since the fix adds `ctx->lc[cnum].last_ntok = 0;` at the start of the token loop.

Actually, wait. Let me re-read the fix more carefully:

```c
+    if (tnum < 0) goto err;
     ctx->desc[tnum<<4].buf = malloc(nreads);
```

This is in the `(ttype & 15) != 0 && (ttype & 128)` block. If `tnum` is -1 (before any `ttype & 128` is seen), then `tnum<<4` is -16, and `ctx->desc[-16]` is an out-of-bounds access. This could corrupt the heap.

But wait, `tnum` starts at -1. The first descriptor must have `ttype & 128` to increment `tnum` to 0. If the first descriptor has `ttype & 64` but NOT `ttype & 128`, then `tnum` stays at -1. Then `i = (tnum<<4) | (ttype&15) = (-16) | (ttype&15)`. Since `ttype&15` is in [0, 15], `i` is in [-16, -1]. Then `ctx->desc[i]` is an out-of-bounds access.

But the check `if (j >= i) goto err;` - if `j` is also negative (from `in[o++]<<4`), then `j >= i` might be false, and we proceed to access `ctx->desc[i]` with negative index.

Actually, `j = in[o++]<<4; j += in[o++];`. `in[o++]` is a `uint8_t`, so `j` is in [0, 4095]. And `i = (tnum<<4) | (ttype&15)`. If `tnum = -1`, then `tnum<<4 = -16`, and `i = -16 | (ttype&15)`. Since `ttype&15` is in [0, 15], `i` is in [-16, -1]. So `j >= i` is always true (since `j >= 0 > i`). So we goto err. No out-of-bounds access.

Hmm, but what if `tnum` is -1 and `ttype & 64` is NOT set? Then we go to the "Load compressed block" path:
```c
if (ttype & 128) {
    tnum++;
    ...
}
```

If `ttype & 128` is set, `tnum` becomes 0. If not, `tnum` stays -1. Then:
```c
i = (tnum<<4) | (ttype&15);
if (i >= MAX_TBLOCKS || i < 0)
    goto err;
```

If `tnum = -1`, then `i = -16 | (ttype&15)`, which is negative. So `i < 0` and we goto err. Fine.

OK so the `tnum < 0` fix is about preventing negative index access, but the existing checks already handle it.

Let me look at the `decode_name` function's `N_DUP` case and the `last_ntok` fix more carefully. The fix adds `ctx->lc[cnum].last_ntok = 0;` at the start of the token loop. This means `last_ntok` is reset to 0 for each new name. Then in `N_DUP`:

```c
ctx->lc[cnum].last_ntok = ctx->lc[pnum].last_ntok;
int nc = ctx->lc[cnum].last_ntok ? ctx->lc[cnum].last_ntok : MAX_TOKENS;
memcpy(ctx->lc[cnum].last_token_type, ctx->lc[pnum].last_token_type, nc * sizeof(int));
```

If `ctx->lc[pnum].last_ntok` is 0 (because `pnum`'s name was never decoded), then `nc = MAX_TOKENS = 128`. The `memcpy` copies 128 * 4 = 512 bytes. That's within bounds.

But in the vulnerable code, `ctx->lc[pnum].last_ntok` might be uninitialized garbage. If it's a huge value, `nc` is huge, and the `memcpy` overflows.

Wait, but `last_ntok` is set in `N_END`:
```c
case N_END:
    ...
    ctx->lc[cnum].last_ntok = ntok;
    return len;
```

So `last_ntok` is set when a name is fully decoded. If `pnum`'s name was decoded, `last_ntok` is valid. If not, it's uninitialized.

But `create_context` initializes `ctx->lc`:
```c
ctx->lc = (last_context *)(((char *)ctx) + sizeof(*ctx));
```

The `last_context` array is part of the `name_context` allocation. It's NOT zeroed. So `last_ntok` is uninitialized.

The fix adds `ctx->lc[0].last_ntok = 0;` in `create_context`. But what about `ctx->lc[1]`, `ctx->lc[2]`, etc.? They're still uninitialized. But the fix also adds `ctx->lc[cnum].last_ntok = 0;` at the start of the token loop in `decode_name`. So each `lc[cnum]` is initialized before use.

OK, so the vulnerability is:
1. `decode_name` is called with `cnum` starting at 0.
